Stop holding the deck twice, and say which allocation is about to fail.

TWO THINGS, FOUND THE SAME WAY.  A deck that dies in the kernel OOM killer
never reaches u_calloc's own error path, so ccx tells you nothing about what
it was trying to allocate.  CCX_ALLOC_TRACE=<MB> logs every allocation at or
above that size -- variable, size, and the source line that asked for it --
BEFORE it is attempted.  On the LCOL4 0.0060 undrained cell that turned a
silent kill into a list, and the list had an obvious entry in it.

  u_calloc.c    CCX_ALLOC_TRACE=<MB>, on the way in
  u_realloc.c   the same
  readinput.c   the entry it found

inpc IS THE DECK, HELD AS TEXT, PADDED TO 132 BYTES A LINE.  readinput grows
it 1.1x while reading, then finishes with

    RENEW(inpc,char,132**nline);

which reserves the maximum line length for every line whatever it actually
holds.  On the 0.0060 deck that is 3.110 GB against ~1.7 GB of content.  It
is not transient either: inpc is freed with ipoinp/inp at the very end of
ccx_2.23.c, after the last step, so the slack is held through the whole
factorisation.

Every reader goes through the offset table -- getnewline.f walks
ipoinpc(iline-1)+1 .. ipoinpc(iline), and the only fixed 132 strides in the
tree are on textpart, a different array -- so ipoinpc[*nline] is exactly the
number of characters stored, and sizing to it is the same deck in less space:

    L_mesh 0.0060 undrained, 1.7 GB deck
      before   grows to 1.734 GB, then RENEW UP to 3.110 GB
      after    grows to 1.734 GB, then RENEW DOWN to 1.592 GB

1.52 GB, on a run whose problem is that it does not fit.

VERIFIED ANSWER-NEUTRAL on the 0.0240 acceptance deck: F_x = 1.552992E+07 in
double, unchanged to every digit.

The trace is off unless the variable is set, and costs one comparison per
allocation when it is.  What it reported on 0.0060, for the record:

  realloc inpc          1.592 GB   readinput.c:488
  calloc  kon           0.768 GB   ccx_2.23.c:237
  calloc  prestr        0.671 GB   ccx_2.23.c:440
  calloc  sti           0.671 GB   ccx_2.23.c:722
  calloc  eme           0.671 GB   ccx_2.23.c:726
  realloc irow          1.511 GB   mastructdedup.c:241
  calloc  xstiff        3.019 GB   linstatic.c:334
  realloc au            6.043 GB   linstatic.c:475
  calloc  au1           6.043 GB   mafillsmasmain.c:207
  calloc  aupardiso     6.071 GB   pardiso.c:302

No single allocation is pathological; the deck is simply large.  Note that
prestr, sti and eme are sized over ALL elements, and on an F-bar deck most
elements are U2/U3 smoothing domains that carry no stress at all -- that is
~2 GB allocated for elements that never write to it, and is left alone here.
